<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/5th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second Persian invasion of Greece</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/480-bce">480 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as invasion of Greece · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-persian-wars">Greco-Persian Wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second Persian invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> was the massive campaign launched by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> in 480 BCE. It included the Persian victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a> and the sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, but Greek victories at Salamis and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a> forced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> to withdraw. This failure revealed the limits of Persian power against Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">hoplites</a>, and accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> also refer to it.</p></div>
